;; EXPECT: ok
;; DERIVE_AC_EQS: on
;; Book chapter 16: with eager AC completion the solver reaches the AC optimum.
;;
;; Byte-for-byte the program of `16-au-plain.egg`, run with `--derive-ac-eqs`. The
;; rebuild that happens before the solver takes its snapshot now runs completion, so
;; `n` and `add(c, d)` are one class by the time the search starts. There is no
;; position at which the two sides disagree, and the reported anti-unifier is the
;; shared term itself:
;;
;;   (anti-unify :size 2 :cr 0.0000 :completion exact
;;     (g n))
;;
;; Size 5 became size 2 and the `Variants` node is gone. Nothing about the solver
;; changed. What changed is the equality relation it was reasoning over.
;;
;; This is the rule to take away: if you are anti-unifying over AC operators and you
;; care whether a reported difference is real, run with eager completion. Otherwise
;; the solver's optimality claim is about a weaker equality than the one you have in
;; mind, and it will over-report.

(sort E)
(function add (E) E :assoc :comm)
(function g (E) E)
(function a () E)
(function b () E)
(function c () E)
(function d () E)
(function n () E)

(union (add a b) c)
(union (add a b d) n)

(let l (g n))
(let r (g (add c d)))

(antiunify l r :algorithm exact)

;; Reached.
(checkau l r :max_size 2 :algorithm exact)
